  {
   "title": "Procedure text: HIGH VOLTAGE board repair with a standard screwdriver (one of the 4 scripts subjects followed)",
   "video_ref": null,
   "duration_s": null,
   "clip": null,
   "clip_offset_s": 0,
   "thumb": null,
   "timeline": [],
   "raw_format": "txt",
   "raw": "1. Sit at the workbench;\n2. Pronounce the voice command \"Record\" to start the recording, a beep will be heard to confirm the operation.                                     \n3. Turn on and off the lamp on the bench while looking at it;\n4. Leave the laboratory;                                   \n5. Enter the laboratory and closes the door;\n6. Head towards panel A and observes it for a few moments;                                     \n7. Press the two green buttons on panel A;\n8. Head towards the workbench and sits down;                                  \n9. Observe the high voltage board for a few moments;\n10. Take the high voltage board and places it on the work area;                                  \n11. Remove the screws from the workbench using the screwdriver;                                   \n12. Fix the board to the workbench using the screwdriver;                                 \n13. Observe the power supply of the board;\n14. Turn on the power supply using the turret switch;                                 \n15. Adjust the current knob of the power supply until the green LED lights up;\n16. Adjust the voltage knob of the power supply setting a voltage of 5 Volts;                                 \n17. Connect the power supply cables to the power points of the board;\n18. Keep an eye on the board for a few seconds to check that the red and green LEDs light up;                                      \n19. Turn off the power supply of the board using the turret switch and waits for the LEDs on the board to go off;\n20. Return the current and voltage knobs of the power supply to 0;                                       \n21. Disconnect the power supply cables;\n22. Remove the board from the workbench using the screwdriver;                                       \n23. Observe the soldering iron;\n24. Turn on the soldering iron using the turret switch;\n25. Set the temperature of the soldering iron to 200 degrees using the yellow button \"UP\";                                       \n26. Grab with pliers the resistor on the board;                                       \n27. Take the tip of the soldering iron;\n28. Touch with the tip of soldering iron, first pin of resistor for 5 seconds;                                      \n29. Touch with tip of soldering iron, second pin of resistor for 5 seconds;                                       \n30. Put down pliers on workbench;\n31. Put down tip of soldering iron;                                      \n32. Put board vertically;\n33. Grab with pliers resistor on board;                                       \n34. Take tip of soldering iron;\n35. Touch with tip of soldering iron, first pin of resistor on back of board for 5 seconds;                                        \n36. Touch with tip of soldering iron, second pin of resistor on back of board for 5 seconds;                                      \n37. Put down tip of soldering iron;\n38. Put down board on work area;\n39. Put down pliers on workbench;                                       \n40. Return to minimum temperature of soldering iron (160 degrees) using yellow button \"DOWN\";\n41. Turn off soldering iron using turret switch;                                      \n42. Fix board to workbench using screwdriver;                                       \n43. Observe power supply of board;\n44. Turn on power supply using turret switch;                                       \n45. Adjust current knob of power supply until green LED lights up;\n46. Adjust voltage knob of power supply setting a voltage of 5 Volts;\n47. Connect the power supply cables to the power points of the board;\n48. Keep an eye on the board for a few seconds to check that the red and green LEDs light up;                                    \n49. Turn on the oscilloscope using the turret switch;\n50. Activate the channel of the probe number 2 using the CH2 MENU button with blue outline;                                       \n51. Connect the ground clip of the probe to test point number 1;                                       \n52. Use the tip of the probe to check some signal carried to test point number 2 on the board;\n53. Press the \"Auto Set\" button of the oscilloscope;                                       \n54. Observe the display of the oscilloscope;\n55. Rotate the \"position\" knob above the CH2 MENU button;\n56. Repeat the previous three points for all remaining test points (from number 3 to number 7);                                        \n57. Turn off the power supply of the board using the turret switch and waits for the LEDs on the board to go off;\n58. Return the current and voltage knobs of the power supply to 0;                                       \n59. Disconnect the ground clip and tip of the oscilloscope;\n60. Disconnect the power supply cables;                                       \n61. Deactivate the channel of the probe number 2;\n62. Turn off the oscilloscope using the turret switch;                                       \n63. Remove the board from the workbench using the screwdriver;                                       \n64. Observe the soldering iron;\n65. Turn on the soldering iron using the turret switch;\n66. Set the temperature of the soldering iron to 200 degrees using the yellow button \"UP\";                                      \n67. Grab with pliers resistor on board;                                       \n68. Take tip of soldering iron;\n69. Touche with tip of soldering iron, first pin of resistor for 5 seconds;                                       \n70. Touche with tip of soldering iron, second pin of resistor for 5 seconds;                                       \n71. Put down pliers on workbench;\n72. Put down tip of soldering iron;                                      \n73. Put board vertically;\n74. Grab with pliers resistor on board;                                      \n75. Take tip of soldering iron;\n76. Touch with tip of soldering iron, first pi